node_modules/
# .env and any hand-rolled backup of it carry a live API_KEY. This repo is
# public, so an untracked .env* in the working tree is one `git add -A` away
# from publishing a credential. Ignore the whole class rather than one spelling
# (.env.bak, .env.old, .env.backup, .env.2 are all just as likely as
# .env.bak-<date>), then re-admit the tracked template.
.env*
!.env.example
# Sparkle writes .sparkle/merge-policy.json into every agent worktree it cuts.
# Nothing in this repo reads it and it is per-worktree, but while it was
# unignored it left every agent worktree permanently dirty — which is enough for
# worktree teardown to refuse ("holds uncommitted changes") and strand the
# worktree. Ignore the directory rather than the one file: the marker set is the
# app's to change, not this repo's.
.sparkle/
.machine_config_hash
.reporting-state.json
.agentsview-update-check
dist/
*.tsbuildinfo
